What is CI/CD, and what tools have I used
CI (Continuous Integration) means team members merge code into the main branch frequently, with every merge automatically triggering a build and test run, so integration conflicts and bugs get caught early instead of piling up before release. CD has two flavors: Continuous Delivery means code that passes all tests is automatically packaged into a releasable artifact, but the final production deployment may still require a manual click; Continuous Deployment automates that last step too. My team used Continuous Delivery — we kept a manual approval gate before production deploys, since it involves real user traffic and the team preferred to stay conservative there.
As for tools, I've used Jenkins for pipeline orchestration, GitHub as the code repo and trigger source, Docker for building images, ECR for storing images, and ECS as the deployment target. For testing, we used SonarQube for code quality scans and Trivy for image vulnerability scanning. I've also worked with GitHub Actions — the difference is it's SaaS-based and tightly coupled with the repo, so you don't need to maintain your own server; Jenkins requires you to set up and maintain it yourself, but it has a much more mature plugin ecosystem and stronger customization for complex pipelines. We chose Jenkins mainly due to legacy reasons plus compliance requirements around self-hosted deployment.
How Jenkins works
Jenkins is essentially an automation server, and its core pattern is "listen for events → trigger a job → run pipeline steps." We configured a webhook on GitHub, so when code is pushed or a PR is merged into a target branch, GitHub sends an HTTP request to Jenkins, which pulls the latest code and runs the stages defined in our pipeline: checkout code, install dependencies, run unit tests, build the Docker image, run integration tests, push the image to the registry, then trigger deployment.
Architecturally, Jenkins uses a Master-Agent model — the Master handles scheduling, while actual builds run on Agent nodes, which lets you scale build capacity horizontally and configure different Agents for different environments (e.g., specific JDK versions or OS requirements). The plugin ecosystem is genuinely useful in practice — Slack notification plugins (so the team gets pinged immediately on build failure), SonarQube plugins, Docker plugins — they let Jenkins integrate seamlessly with the rest of the toolchain without writing custom glue scripts.
What is a Jenkinsfile
A Jenkinsfile is the definition file for a Jenkins pipeline — it describes the entire CI/CD flow as code, so the pipeline itself is version-controlled alongside the codebase, which makes it easy to code-review and track changes. There are two syntax styles: Declarative, which has a fixed stage-based structure and is more readable, suited for most standard cases; and Scripted, based on Groovy, which is more flexible for complex conditional logic but harder to maintain. My team defaulted to Declarative and only dropped into Scripted blocks for genuinely complex branching logic (e.g., different deployment targets per branch).
Our Jenkinsfile roughly went: checkout code → install dependencies → run unit tests (fail fast if they don't pass) → build Docker image → run integration tests → push to ECR → deploy to staging → manual approval → deploy to production. Each stage's status is visible in the Jenkins UI, and failures trigger a Slack notification so the team can pinpoint the issue immediately. The Jenkinsfile lives at the repo root, so even if we migrate Jenkins environments, the pipeline logic isn't lost — it's just part of the code.

